        {
          "id": "f1feab63-e023-3774-9ec0-b909adc982b3",
          "content": {
            "id": "f1feab63-e023-3774-9ec0-b909adc982b3",
            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "Tech Weekly: Musk's NASA spat, and remote car drivers",
            "description": "<title><body><p>STORY: From Elon Musk's fight with the head of NASA, to the cars controlled by remote drivers.</p><p>This is Tech Weekly.</p><p>:: Tech Weekly</p><p>SpaceX CEO Elon Musk battled online with the acting NASA boss over who should lead the space agency.</p><p>The billionaire responded negatively to reports Sean Duffy wants to fold NASA into the Transportation Department, which he also runs.</p><p>Musk said the person leading the space agency couldn't have a \"2-digit IQ\".\u00a0</p><p>The comment came after Duffy said SpaceX was behind schedule on a planned mission to return humans to the moon, and other companies could get a chance to bid for the contract instead.\u00a0</p><p>SpaceX could face more competition from a new European rival.</p><p>The region's top space firms - Airbus, Thales and Leonardo - agreed to combine their satellite manufacturing and services businesses.</p><p>They have long competed to put complex spacecraft into geostationary orbits.\u00a0</p><p>Now they want to compete with SpaceX and others, which use smaller, cheaper satellites for services like Starlink.</p><p>A start-up in Berlin has produced what it sees as the future of auto mobility.</p><p>Driverless car sharing company 'Vay' sends electric cars to customers with nobody physically behind the wheel.</p><p>Instead, they're driven by a remote pilot, who takes charge of any challenging parking issues.</p><p>CEO Thomas von der Ohe wants his company to replace private car ownership.\u00a0</p><p>\u201cIf you look into cities where they're clogged with private cars, they are not moving 95% of the time, they're actually standing still and taking place away from us humans living there. We'd love to really create a true alternative to that.\u201d</p><p>The tech is already in use in Las Vegas and being tested in Berlin.</p><p>Meanwhile, Tesla faces a probe over a new driver assistance mode dubbed \"Mad Max\".\u00a0</p><p>The firm says the setting will operate its vehicles at higher speeds, and is the mode to use if you are running late.\u00a0</p><p>But U.S. watchdogs say they're investigating reports the system could cause Tesla cars to exceed speed limits.\u00a0</p><p>And Qualcomm shares leapt more than a tenth to hit a yearly high Monday (October 27).</p><p>Investors bought in after the chipmaker unveiled two new AI chips for data centers.</p><p>The moves diversify the tech giant beyond a stagnant smartphone market.</p><p>And help it take on Nvidia's dominance of data centers.</p></body></title>",
            "summary": "<body><p>STORY: From Elon Musk's fight with the head of NASA, to the cars controlled by remote drivers.</p><p>This is Tech Weekly.</p><p>:: Tech Weekly</p><p>SpaceX CEO Elon Musk battled online with the acting NASA boss over who should lead the space agency.</p><p>The billionaire responded negatively to reports Sean Duffy wants to fold NASA into the Transportation Department, which he also runs.</p><p>Musk said the person leading the space agency couldn't have a \"2-digit IQ\".&nbsp;</p><p>The comment came after Duffy said SpaceX was behind schedule on a planned mission to return humans to the moon, and other companies could get a chance to bid for the contract instead.&nbsp;</p><p>SpaceX could face more competition from a new European rival.</p><p>The region's top space firms - Airbus, Thales and Leonardo - agreed to combine their satellite manufacturing and services businesses.</p><p>They have long competed to put complex spacecraft into geostationary orbits.&nbsp;</p><p>Now they want to compete with SpaceX and others, which use smaller, cheaper satellites for services like Starlink.</p><p>A start-up in Berlin has produced what it sees as the future of auto mobility.</p><p>Driverless car sharing company 'Vay' sends electric cars to customers with nobody physically behind the wheel.</p><p>Instead, they're driven by a remote pilot, who takes charge of any challenging parking issues.</p><p>CEO Thomas von der Ohe wants his company to replace private car ownership.&nbsp;</p><p>\u201cIf you look into cities where they're clogged with private cars, they are not moving 95% of the time, they're actually standing still and taking place away from us humans living there. We'd love to really create a true alternative to that.\u201d</p><p>The tech is already in use in Las Vegas and being tested in Berlin.</p><p>Meanwhile, Tesla faces a probe over a new driver assistance mode dubbed \"Mad Max\".&nbsp;</p><p>The firm says the setting will operate its vehicles at higher speeds, and is the mode to use if you are running late.&nbsp;</p><p>But U.S. watchdogs say they're investigating reports the system could cause Tesla cars to exceed speed limits.&nbsp;</p><p>And Qualcomm shares leapt more than a tenth to hit a yearly high Monday (October 27).</p><p>Investors bought in after the chipmaker unveiled two new AI chips for data centers.</p><p>The moves diversify the tech giant beyond a stagnant smartphone market.</p><p>And help it take on Nvidia's dominance of data centers.</p></body>",
